===South America===

====Argentina====
In Argentina,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.mercedes-benz.com.ar/passengercars/being-an-owner/service-hotline.html|title=Servicio de atención al cliente Mercedes-Benz|website=www.mercedes-benz.com.ar}}</ref> the Fortwo has been for sale since 2010 and models (Fortwo Cabrio and Fortwo Coupé) can be bought in dealerships located in the [[Puerto Madero]] neighborhood of [[Buenos Aires]].

====Brazil====
In Brazil,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.smart.com/br/pt/index/smart-fortwo.html|title=Brazil homepage}}</ref> the Fortwo has been for sale since 2009 and models (Fortwo Cabrio turbo, Fortwo Coupé turbo and Fortwo Coupé MHD) can be bought in some Smart and/or Mercedes-Benz dealerships in [[São Paulo]], [[Rio de Janeiro]], [[Belo Horizonte]] and [[Porto Alegre]] cities. The electric, Brabus and Forfour versions are not available for the Brazilian market.

Smart was launched in Brazil in 2009, but stopped being sold in 2016.<ref>{{Cite web |last=G1 |first=Do |last2=Paulo |first2=em São |date=2016-02-15 |title=Smart some das lojas e marca não confirma nova geração no Brasil |url=https://g1.globo.com/carros/noticia/2016/02/smart-some-das-lojas-e-marca-nao-confirma-nova-geracao-no-brasil.html |access-date=2025-05-10 |website=Auto Esporte |language=pt-br}}</ref> 

Eventually, Smart is planning a return to Brazil by 2026, with electric cars.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Smart prepara retorno ao Brasil até 2026 com carros elétricos |url=https://quatrorodas.abril.com.br/carros-eletricos/smart-prepara-retorno-ao-brasil-ate-2026-com-carros-eletricos/ |access-date=2025-05-10 |website=Quatro Rodas |language=pt-BR}}</ref>

===Oceania===

====Australia====
Smart models were for sale in Australia from 2003 to 2015. It was announced in March 2015 that the Smart brand would be withdrawn from Australia due to poor sales, however, it was announced in May 2024 that the Smart brand would re-enter the Australian market.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.news.com.au/finance/business/smart-car-brand-axed-in-australia-due-to-slow-sales/story-fnkgdhrc-1227249971610|title=Smart car brand axed in Australia due to slow sales|work=NewsComAu|access-date=14 July 2015}}</ref> 

===Europe===
In 2004, the brand was launched in Malta, Norway and Romania.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://group.mercedes-benz.com/documents/investors/berichte/geschaeftsberichte/daimlerchrysler/daimler-ir-annualreport-2004.pdf|title=Annual Report 2004}}</ref>

====United Kingdom====
Launched in the United Kingdom in 2000, the current range features the [[Smart Fortwo]] convertible, and Smart Fortwo Coupé. Smart is available in the UK through [[Mercedes-Benz]] retailers.

The UK is host to a number of annual events, both official and unofficial, including the Smart Festival, held annually at Mercedes-Benz World in Weybridge, near historic [[Brooklands]] - the world's first purpose-built motor racing circuit.

====Russia====
The Smart brand debuted in the [[Russia]]n market in 2012 with the [[Smart Fortwo|Fortwo]] model only.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://wroom.ru/en/news/466 |title=Smart Enters Russian Market |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date=20 June 2012 |website=Wroom.ru |access-date=14 October 2014}}</ref>

==Safety==
[[File:smart Car Structure.JPG|thumb|right|[[Cutaway (industrial)|Cutaway]] showing structure of the smart Fortwo]]
The Smart Fortwo uses a very small front [[crumple zone]]. The second generation Smart Fortwo has been awarded 4 out of 5 stars in the [[Euro NCAP]] Adult Occupant Protection and 2 out of 4 stars in the Pedestrian protection test, but was not tested for Child Occupant Protection, as it has no rear seats.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.euroncap.com/tests/smart_Fortwo_2007/303.aspx |title=Smart Fortwo test results |access-date=2008-04-26 |publisher=Euro NCAP}}</ref> The original Smart was awarded 3 out of 5 stars for Adult Occupant Protection.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.euroncap.com/tests/smart_city_coupe_2000/29.aspx |title=Smart City Coupe test results |access-date=2008-04-26 |publisher=Euro NCAP}}</ref> In American tests using a five-star rating, Smart cars received a four-star safety rating for the driver from a front impact, and a five-star safety rating for the driver for a side impact.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.safercar.gov/portal/site/safercar/menuitem.db847bd57e3dc1f885dfc38c35a67789/?vgnextoid=c95df2905bf54110VgnVCM1000002fd17898RCRD&model=7473 |title=Smart fortwo crash ratings |access-date=2008-04-30 |publisher=Safercar.gov}}</ref>  It also received "Good" ratings for front and side crash protection in Insurance Institute for Highway Safety (IIHS) tests.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.iihs.org/news/rss/pr051408.html |title=First Institute crash tests of Smart car: diminutive two-seater earns top ratings for protecting people in front & side crashes |access-date=2008-05-14 |publisher=Insurance Institute for Highway Safety}}</ref>  However, in an April 2009 {{convert|40|mph|km/h|adj=on}} frontal offset crash test between a Fortwo and a Mercedes C-Class, "the Smart went air-borne and turned around 450 degrees" causing "extensive intrusion into the space around the dummy from head to feet". The IIHS rated the Smart Fortwo "Poor," noting that "Multiple injuries, including to the head, would be likely for a real-world driver of a smart in a similar collision."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.iihs.org/externaldata/srdata/docs/sr4404.pdf |title=IIHS Status Report, Special Issue: Car Size, Weight, and Safety |access-date=2009-04-17 |publisher=Insurance Institute for Highway Safety}}</ref>

The main structure of the car is a stiff structure, marketed as the Tridion Safety Cell, designed to activate the crumple zones of a colliding vehicle. This design creates a safety cell around the passengers, according to the manufacturer.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.smartusa.com/smart-Fortwo-safety-design.aspx |title=Smart fortwo safety design |access-date=2008-04-26 }}</ref>

